# ── Vital environment variables ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values there.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must never be committed: it is where secrets live.
# Everything below is SERVER-SIDE ONLY (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix), so none of these
# values can reach the browser bundle. Placeholders only — never a real token.
#
# With nothing set, Vital runs in demo mode over the committed fixtures.

# ── Host / container ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port the container is published on (container always listens on 3000).
# Change it if 8080 is taken:  VITAL_PORT=9090 docker compose up -d
VITAL_PORT=8080

# Node heap cap for the container runtime, in MB. Keep it small on tight hosts.
NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=384

# Server-side fallback timezone, used only until a profile is stored. The first
# browser visit stores that browser's timezone in the profile, and Settings →
# Account changes it; the profile's timezone is what cuts the calendar days.
# VITAL_TIMEZONE=America/Chicago

# ── Data source ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `live`  — read the Health Auto Export history, server-side, on every request
#           (cached in-process). The browser never calls this API and never sees
#           the token. If the source cannot be read, the app shows a connection
#           error; it never substitutes demo data.
# `demo`  — serve the committed fixtures (src/data/health-fixtures.json).
# Leave unset for demo mode.
# VITAL_DATA_MODE=live

# ── Health Auto Export integration ────────────────────────────────────────────

# The server address and read key are NOT set here. Enter them in
# Settings → Sources: they are checked, then stored encrypted in Postgres
# under VITAL_SECRET_KEY (below). `npm run db:init` generates that key.

# Metric the pipeline panel probes (over a bounded one-week window) before it
# marks the live stages healthy. Defaults to resting_heart_rate.
# HAE_PROBE_METRIC=resting_heart_rate

# How long a fetched, normalized dataset is cached in the server process, in
# seconds. Concurrent page loads share one upstream pass, and the cache is warmed
# once at process start. Once this TTL lapses the held dataset is served stale
# while one background refresh runs, so a page never waits for the rebuild.
# Defaults to 300.
# HAE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# ── Oura Ring (optional second live source) ──────────────────────────────────
# Register an app at https://cloud.ouraring.com/oauth/applications. Personal
# access tokens no longer exist; Vital uses OAuth2 with PKCE. The client ID,
# client secret and redirect URI are entered in Settings → Sources and
# stored encrypted; they are not read from the environment. Only the encrypted
# values and tokens are stored; no Oura reading is ever written to the
# database, a file or a log. The values below are admin settings.
# Add heart_health to read VO2 max (Oura names the scope in its developer portal as "Heart Health").
# OURA_SCOPES=daily heartrate workout spo2
# OURA_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300        # same meaning as HAE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS
# OURA_HEARTRATE_LOOKBACK_DAYS=30   # heart rate is ~288 samples a day; read less history
# OURA_HEARTRATE_CHUNK_DAYS=7
# Metric groups where the ring wins over the watch. Comma list of:
# sleep, recovery, activity, heart, workouts
# OURA_PREFERRED_FOR=sleep,recovery
# Override only for tests or the Oura sandbox:
# OURA_API_URL=https://api.ouraring.com
#
# Key that encrypts the stored connections (the Health Auto Export key and the
# OAuth tokens): 32 random bytes, base64. `npm run db:init` generates one when
# it is absent. To make one by hand:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Required to save any connection (Health Auto Export, Oura, Hevy).
# VITAL_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Workout sources ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Apple Health only records a workout's type, time and calories. A workout source
# adds the exercises, sets, reps, load and RPE that the routine on the Workouts
# page tracks. Sessions are synced into server memory only (never the database),
# and only in live mode — demo mode serves committed demo sessions.
#
# Hevy (https://api.hevyapp.com/docs/) — needs Hevy Pro; create a key at
# https://hevy.com/settings?developer. The first sync backfills the lookback
# window; later syncs read Hevy's change feed (/v1/workouts/events).
# The key and address are entered in Settings → Connections → Workout sources, not here.
# HEVY_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300
#
# How far back every source backfills, in days. Defaults to 400.
# WORKOUT_SOURCE_LOOKBACK_DAYS=400

# ── Activity maps ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Routes are read once per workout from Health Auto Export and held in server
# memory only. Cap on route points held (about 13 bytes each); the least recently
# used routes are dropped beyond it.
# ROUTE_CACHE_MAX_POINTS=3000000
#
# Place search in the add-map dialog. The typed text is sent from the server to
# this geocoder; "off" disables search ("lat, lon" and "use my location" still
# work). Nominatim asks for an identifying User-Agent with a contact.
# GEOCODER_URL=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search
# GEOCODER_USER_AGENT=Vital (self-hosted; you@example.org)
#
# Map tile providers. OpenStreetMap and OpenTopoMap need no key. CARTO needs
# one (free for non-commercial use: https://carto.com/basemaps/apikey); a map
# set to CARTO without it requests its tiles keyless (they may not load) and
# says so. Tile keys are public by design: this one is handed to the browser
# for its tile URLs. Settings → Connections → Map sources shows which are ready.
# MAP_TILES_CARTO_KEY=

# ── Profile ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The profile is NOT configured with environment variables: it is a JSON file the
# server owns and writes, so it is the same for every browser and both the
# greeting and the briefing read one record.
#
#   ./data/profile.json          on the host
#   /app/data/profile.json       inside the container (docker-compose mounts ./data)
#
# Fields: name, dateOfBirth (YYYY-MM-DD), notes, timezone (IANA), briefingHour
# (0-23). All optional; no credential and no health record belongs in it. It is
# edited in Settings → Account, or by editing the file directly.
# When the file does not exist the documented defaults are used and nothing
# crashes. data/profile.example.json is the shape reference.
#
# Optional: put the file somewhere else (it must be writable by the server process
# and should be a mounted volume, or the contents are lost on redeploy).
# VITAL_PROFILE_PATH=/app/data/profile.json
#
# The host directory the container writes into must be writable by uid 1001:
#   mkdir -p data && chmod 777 data

# ── AI providers and models ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set up in the app, not here: Settings → Connections → AI providers holds each
# model server (address and API key, encrypted with VITAL_SECRET_KEY), and
# Settings → Preferences → AI models chooses the provider and model for the
# Analyst, the Summary (today's briefing, training notes) and the Summary
# fallback, with their tuning. With nothing chosen the analyst is a
# deterministic demo and the summary is computed: nothing is sent anywhere.
#
# The old ANALYST_PROVIDER / _MODEL / _API_URL / _API_KEY, VITAL_LLM_* and the
# other tuning variables are no longer read (see docs/configuration.md).
#
# Only the system prompt and the timeouts stay deployment settings:
#
# System prompt. ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE WINS over the inline value when both
# are set. The file is re-read at request time when its mtime changes, so editing
# it takes effect on the next question. The container mounts ./config read-only
# at /app/config, and config/analyst-prompt.md is committed as a starting point.
# When neither is set the built-in prompt is used.
# ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT=You are a laconic analyst. Answer only from the context.
# ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE=/app/config/analyst-prompt.md
#
# Timeouts (defaults shown). One request to the model; one whole analyst
# question, every tool round included; one briefing request (it runs in the
# background, so it is shorter).
# ANALYST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# ANALYST_QUESTION_TIMEOUT_MS=300000
# BRIEFING_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
#
# There is deliberately NO briefing cache TTL to set. A briefing's cache key is
# the local calendar day (in the profile's timezone) plus the unit system, so one
# briefing is written per day and reused for that whole day; new observations
# arriving during the day do not regenerate it. A new day's briefing is written
# lazily, by the first request at/after the profile's briefingHour — there is no
# scheduler and no background job. `Regenerate` on the hero replaces the current
# day's briefing on demand.

# ── Postgres: configuration, profile and (later) analyst conversations ────────
# NOT health data. No observations, metric series or workouts are ever stored in
# this database; health history stays with the Health Auto Export source.
#
# Generate these once with `npm run db:init` (scripts/init-env.sh): it creates a
# strong password the first time, appends anything missing, and never overwrites
# an existing value. `docker compose up -d --build` then starts the database.
#
# HOST-SIDE values: used by psql and `npm run db:migrate` run on this machine.
# 5432 is deliberately NOT used — another Postgres on this host already owns it.
VITAL_PG_HOST=127.0.0.1
VITAL_PG_PORT=5433
VITAL_PG_DATABASE=vital
VITAL_PG_USER=vital
VITAL_PG_PASSWORD=          # generated by `npm run db:init`; never committed
VITAL_PG_SSL=false
#
# A full connection string wins over the parts above when both are set.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://vital:password@127.0.0.1:5433/vital
#
# INSIDE the compose network the app connects to the service directly, so
# docker-compose.yml passes it separate values (host `vital-postgres`, port
# 5432). Override only if you rename the service or move the database off-host:
# VITAL_PG_INTERNAL_HOST / VITAL_PG_INTERNAL_PORT.
#
# With none of these set, the app runs on its JSON files exactly as before.

# ── Uploaded lab reports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An uploaded lab PDF is stored CONTENT-ADDRESSED (named after the SHA-256 of its
# own bytes) so the same document uploaded twice occupies one file, and a
# re-parse reads exactly the bytes the hash names. Only the file's hash is used
# to build its path — never the uploaded filename.
#
# `./data` is already mounted writable at /app/data, so the default directory
# lives inside it and survives a redeploy.
# VITAL_LAB_DIR=/app/data/lab

# Largest accepted upload, in bytes. Defaults to 15 MB (15728640).
# VITAL_LAB_MAX_BYTES=15728640

# The OPTIONAL second extraction pass, used only when the deterministic pass
# finds no complete analyte row, is switched in Settings → Preferences → AI
# models → Analyst (VITAL_LAB_MODEL_ASSIST is no longer read). Every value it
# proposes is validated against the document text before it is kept.
